WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.800
Question 0014: random squares and rectangles
without overlap. Junior level.

2
00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.767
Two parts: squares, then
rectangles of different sizes.

3
00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:14.233
A grid of 10 by 8 points: x
from 1 to 10, and y from 1 to 8.

4
00:00:14.233 --> 00:00:19.633
Every shape is given by two points: the
bottom-left corner and the top-right one.

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:25.033
The square from (2, 2) to (5, 5): width 3
and height 3. The rectangle: width 3, height 4.

6
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:30.233
The goal: draw a random number of shapes,
so that no two of them overlap.

7
00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:35.133
They may touch: a shared edge,
part of an edge, or only a shared corner.

8
00:00:35.933 --> 00:00:40.733
Part A: draw a random number of
squares on the grid, without overlap.

9
00:00:42.133 --> 00:00:46.367
Let's add squares one by one.
The first: from (2, 2) to (5, 5).

10
00:00:46.367 --> 00:00:51.467
The next, from (4, 3) to (7, 6), goes into
the first: they share area. Rejected.

11
00:00:51.467 --> 00:00:56.000
From (5, 2) to (7, 4): it touches the first
along part of an edge. Allowed.

12
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:00.733
From (5, 5) to (7, 7): it touches the first
only at one corner. Also allowed.

13
00:01:00.733 --> 00:01:04.533
From (6, 3) to (8, 5): it overlaps the second.
Rejected.

14
00:01:04.533 --> 00:01:09.567
A small square inside the first, from (3, 3)
to (4, 4): it overlaps too. Rejected.

15
00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:13.800
From (7, 2) to (9, 4): a whole shared
edge with the second. Allowed.

16
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.100
So what is forbidden? Only shared area.
Touching along a line or at a point is fine.

17
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.800
It is hard to say directly when two squares
overlap. Easier to say when they don't.

18
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:28.600
Square j does not overlap i if it lies
entirely in one of the four zones around it.

19
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:33.333
Left: x2 of j is at most x1 of i.
The same for right, below and above.

20
00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:37.300
One of them is enough,
so they are joined with "or".

21
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:42.400
The sign is <=, not <: when x2 of j equals
x1 of i, they share an edge. Allowed.

22
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.167
And the other way round: they overlap if and
only if the projections meet on x and on y.

23
00:01:48.167 --> 00:01:52.833
This is the negation of the "or", by De Morgan.
We'll use it in the checker, later.

24
00:01:53.633 --> 00:01:57.300
All the rules together. Count: n from 1
to MAX_SHAPES.

25
00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:02.533
Every shape on the grid, its end right of and
above its start: an edge of at least 1.

26
00:02:02.533 --> 00:02:07.567
A square: width equals height. And for every
pair i < j: one of the four zones.

27
00:02:08.367 --> 00:02:12.833
Now let's write the class
in SystemVerilog, with constraints.

28
00:02:16.833 --> 00:02:20.567
The class: the size of the grid,
and the most shapes, 6.

29
00:02:24.033 --> 00:02:28.533
The random variables: n, and dynamic
arrays for every shape's corners.

30
00:02:36.867 --> 00:02:41.100
The first constraint: n from 1 to 6,
and every array's size equals n.

31
00:02:51.567 --> 00:02:56.033
The second: every shape on the grid,
its end right of and above its start.

32
00:03:02.233 --> 00:03:06.733
The third: width equals height. It stands
alone on purpose, for part B.

33
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:23.100
And the fourth: for every pair i < j, the four
zones joined with "or", with <=.

34
00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:29.000
randomize() solves all the constraints together.
Here is one solution, from the page's examples.

35
00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:33.533
c_count: the solver picks n = 6,
and the four arrays have size 6.

36
00:03:33.533 --> 00:03:38.333
c_on_grid: every shape on the grid,
the second corner right of and above the first.

37
00:03:38.333 --> 00:03:43.000
c_square: each one's width equals
its height. They are all squares.

38
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:48.100
c_no_overlap: 6 shapes, 15 pairs.
In every pair one of the four cases holds.

39
00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.867
Note the touching pairs: 1 and 5, for example,
touch at a corner. The <= allows them.

40
00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:58.367
A trap: without i < j the loop also checks
a shape against itself.

41
00:03:58.667 --> 00:04:02.833
There no case holds, so
randomize() always fails.

42
00:04:03.233 --> 00:04:07.833
A second trap: < instead of <=. Then
touching shapes count as overlapping.

43
00:04:08.133 --> 00:04:12.933
This solution has 7 touching pairs, and all
would be refused, against the requirement.

44
00:04:13.733 --> 00:04:18.700
In DV we don't trust randomize() without
checking: first, that it returned 1.

45
00:04:18.700 --> 00:04:24.000
Then a separate checker, shapes_ok, written
in the "and" form: overlap on both axes.

46
00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:28.533
Two wordings of the same rule: a mistake
in one won't get past the other.

47
00:04:29.333 --> 00:04:33.933
In the page's box: the grid of 10 by 8,
and up to 6 shapes in each draw.

48
00:04:33.933 --> 00:04:38.233
Seed 7: a draw of one shape.
1 is a legal number too.

49
00:04:38.233 --> 00:04:41.367
Seed 12: two squares.

50
00:04:41.367 --> 00:04:45.900
Seed 25: four squares,
three of them touching each other.

51
00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:52.100
The cost: n(n-1)/2 pair constraints.
With 6 shapes, 15 that the solver solves together.

52
00:04:52.899 --> 00:04:57.699
Part B: rectangles of different sizes.
Still no overlap, and touching is allowed.

53
00:04:58.399 --> 00:05:02.866
Which rule belongs only to squares?
Just one: width equals height.

54
00:05:02.866 --> 00:05:07.466
That one goes out. All the rest stay,
the overlap rule too, as it is.

55
00:05:11.566 --> 00:05:16.066
A derived class: rect_set gets
all the constraints of square_set.

56
00:05:18.833 --> 00:05:23.333
A constraint with the same name replaces the
base class's one. An empty body: no rule.

57
00:05:24.833 --> 00:05:29.499
A second way, with no new class: turn
the constraint off in an existing object.

58
00:05:31.099 --> 00:05:33.599
Create an ordinary square_set.

59
00:05:35.866 --> 00:05:40.366
constraint_mode(0) turns c_square off
in this object: now it draws rectangles.

60
00:05:42.199 --> 00:05:44.699
And randomize as usual.

61
00:05:46.199 --> 00:05:51.999
A draw of rectangles, from the page's examples:
six shapes, most touching, no shared area.

62
00:05:52.799 --> 00:05:56.899
Why not check by corners? Here are two
rectangles in the shape of a cross.

63
00:05:56.899 --> 00:06:01.133
No corner of one is inside the other,
and still they share area.

64
00:06:01.133 --> 00:06:06.033
Our rule checks the projections: they
meet on x and on y. They overlap.

65
00:06:06.033 --> 00:06:11.266
With squares a cross is impossible, so only
the rule on the axes is right for both parts.

66
00:06:12.066 --> 00:06:15.566
In the page's box: switch to rectangles,
part B.

67
00:06:15.566 --> 00:06:19.466
Seed 5: five rectangles,
three of them touching.

68
00:06:19.466 --> 00:06:22.599
Seed 9: two rectangles.

69
00:06:23.399 --> 00:06:28.899
In the page's Python solution: 196 possible
squares on the grid, and 1,260 rectangles.

70
00:06:28.899 --> 00:06:33.366
And in SystemVerilog the same cost:
pair constraints, n(n-1)/2.

71
00:06:34.166 --> 00:06:39.266
In short: four zones joined with "or", with <=
so that touching shapes are allowed.

72
00:06:39.266 --> 00:06:44.166
Try draws of your own in the box
on the page of question 0014 on the site.
